WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.880
Show Business can be a sticky business.

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.990
Such is the case with the enormous "The Walking
Dead" series — there's been a legal dispute

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:12.020
ongoing behind the scenes between AMC — the
network which airs the show and its spin-off

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:15.950
prequel, "Fear the Walking Dead" — and Robert
Kirkman, the comic book writer who created

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:17.520
the whole franchise.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:18.520
What's at stake?

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:22.590
Millions of dollars in net profits from rerun
and streaming exhibitions of the show that

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:26.690
Kirkman, along with his co-producers claim
they have not seen in the wake of the show's

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:29.980
major success despite contractual guarantees.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.340
They have challenged the amount that the cable
network has been shelling out to them via

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.210
a breach of contract lawsuit.

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:40.880
While AMC recently settled a suit from former
showrunner Frank Darabont and the talent agency

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:46.700
CAA for a bit over $200 million over "future
revenue sharing related to certain future

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:51.720
streaming exhibition of 'The Walking Dead'
and 'Fear The Walking Dead,'" the suit initiated

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.840
by Kirkman and his co-plaintiffs has lingered
on.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.760
These kinds of conflicts over net receipts
are common in Hollywood, and have only grown

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:04.379
more complex since the advent and rapid proliferation
of streaming, a model not contemplated by

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:06.159
many legacy contracts.

00:01:06.159 --> 00:01:09.829
This case is of particular interest because
of the eye-popping numbers involved.

00:01:09.829 --> 00:01:14.229
"The Walking Dead" is one of the most successful
franchises in television history.

00:01:14.229 --> 00:01:18.799
It's already spawned two spin-off series,
and although the flagship show is about to

00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:24.389
make its final bow with it’s 11th and final
season, AMC seems determined to keep the universe

00:01:24.389 --> 00:01:26.020
alive long into the future.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:31.400
A July 2020 decision initially dealt a blow
to the plaintiffs after Los Angeles Superior

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:36.270
Court Judge Buckley ruled that AMC had correctly
interpreted the terms of the contracts in

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:39.069
regard to the licensing fees owed on seven
counts.

00:01:39.069 --> 00:01:42.469
However, now it appears that a legal change
is in the wind.

00:01:42.469 --> 00:01:49.090
A bit over a year later, July 27th, 2021,
Judge Buckley ruled that Kirkman and his co-plaintiffs

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:54.080
could pursue further punitive damages and
legal theories via two amended claims.

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.969
These claims accuse AMC of "tortious interference"
and "alleged breach of the implied covenant

00:01:58.969 --> 00:02:02.899
of good faith and fair dealing," according
to The Hollywood Reporter.

00:02:02.899 --> 00:02:07.280
To clear up some of the legalese, the second
claim accuses AMC of defining the terms of

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:10.939
profit sharing to guarantee Kirkman and the
others would make no money.

00:02:10.939 --> 00:02:15.379
Meanwhile, the first alleges that AMC acted
deliberately to give its sister company an

00:02:15.379 --> 00:02:17.180
advantage in these matters.

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:21.620
Orin Snyder of the law firm Gibson Dunn explained
the decision to The Hollywood Reporter as

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:23.040
an unimpressive overture:

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:28.349
"It is just as meritless as the 7 out of 7
claims we beat at trial last year, and we

00:02:28.349 --> 00:02:31.400
are confident we will defeat this new claim
as well."

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:35.110
Most creators, like Kirkman, contract for
a percentage of backend receipts in their

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:36.719
original option deals.

00:02:36.719 --> 00:02:41.930
The most popular method of calculating this
compensation is Modified Adjusted Gross Receipts.

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:46.219
There are several standard ways of calculating
it, and it can all get pretty complicated,

00:02:46.219 --> 00:02:51.019
but it's basically how the money is calculated
up and divided among the people legally obligated

00:02:51.019 --> 00:02:52.019
to have it.

00:02:52.019 --> 00:02:56.959
Long story short, the bad faith that Kirkman
alleges is that AMC always intended its calculation

00:02:56.959 --> 00:03:01.319
to leave him with as little as possible thanks
to “creative calculations.”

00:03:01.319 --> 00:03:05.510
While Snyder is correct to point out that
this preliminary ruling doesn't have any bearing

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:09.579
on the legal merits of Kirkman's case — and
what do you expect him to say he represents

00:03:09.579 --> 00:03:14.120
AMC — the judge did see enough evidence
of malice as alleged in the complaint to proceed

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:15.120
to trial.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.469
If Kirkman can show by a preponderance of
the evidence that AMC did contract in bad

00:03:19.469 --> 00:03:24.290
faith, and never intended its calculation
to yield any proceeds whatsoever, he might

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:26.159
just land a favorable verdict.

00:03:26.159 --> 00:03:31.280
That verdict would set a very dangerous precedent
for the entire industry, and might have other

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:36.990
creators unsatisfied with their paltry backend
checks looking up the name of a good attorney.

